# Test: `ze request wireguard keypair` is dispatched and takes no arguments
# Exercises: registry local command "request wireguard keypair" -> RunWgKeypair
#            (internal/plugins/diag/diag.go, registered in register.go)
# VALIDATES: the three-word command resolves through the local-command registry in
#            the real ze binary, its argument guard rejects a stray token, and the
#            refusal names the command and prints the usage.
# PREVENTS:  a command that resolves nowhere, and a guard that lets an argument
#            through to key generation.
#
# The SUCCESS path is NOT here. Ze makes the keys in process, with no `wg`
# involved. TestRunWgKeypair_GeneratesInProcess
# (internal/plugins/diag/diag_test.go) runs it with an empty PATH and derives
# the public key from the private key independently, and the local-data
# coverage walk (test/ui/pipe-local-command.ci, runWireGuardKeypair in
# internal/test/localdatacoverage/tool_commands.go) runs it through the real
# ze binary and checks the same derivation on its JSON answer.

stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONF
# Minimal valid config; the command below never reads it.
bgp {
	router-id 203.0.113.253
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65533
				remote 65533
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# A stray token must be refused BEFORE RunWgKeypair makes a key. That is what
# the stderr text and the stdout rejection discriminate: drop the guard and a
# printed keypair appears on stdout with exit code 0.
cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze request wireguard keypair extra-arg:exit=1
expect=exit:code=1
expect=stderr:contains=request wireguard keypair: no arguments accepted
expect=stderr:contains=Usage: ze request wireguard keypair
reject=stdout:contains=private:
